# Composed modules allowed to consume the module-facing capability surface, as
# a JSON map of module prefix — the same identity the module federates with — to
# the authority its service principal holds:
#
#   {"documents": {"queues": ["datasource"], "namespaces": ["document"],
#                  "resources": ["documents"],
#                  "workload": {"service_account": "documents",
#                               "namespace": "<kubernetes namespace>",
#                               "container": "app"},
#                  "cross_tenant": true, "tenant": "<org uuid>"}}
#
# The principal id is derived from the prefix, so nothing here is hand-authored
# as an opaque id — an entry keyed by a principal id is rejected. `queues` bounds
# the queues the module may enqueue to and claim from, `namespaces` the event
# namespaces it may publish into, `resources` the permission resource types the
# module's own content is governed by — which is how this host authorizes reads
# of content it does not hold, without naming a consumer's domain noun in its own
# code, and which bounds the records the module may place at a scope node — and
# `tenant` the org its Work Context is bound to, which is required and
# must be an organization id.
#
# `workload` is REQUIRED and all three of its parts are: it is the Kubernetes
# identity the module's pods run as, and the container within them that runs its
# image. Execution binding is unconditional, so this is what authenticates a
# module mint — the host reviews the caller's projected service-account token
# and refuses when the identity it attests is not the one declared here. Without
# it the prefix above would be a claim nothing checks, and a module holding its
# own valid token could mint as any other module it shares an image with. The
# container is declared rather than supplied by the caller because a pod's
# containers do not all run the same image, so naming one picks which image the
# approval is tested against. An entry missing any part of it fails the boot,
# naming the entry. `cross_tenant` lets an
# inbox worker act on tenants other than that one, which is what servicing every
# tenant's deliveries needs. Empty means no module may call the surface.
# `read_audiences` and `operation_audiences` are installation-owned exchange
# policy: each binding fixes its target audience and canonical scopes. A caller
# supplies only a binding id (plus the operation binding's lookup selector),
# never an audience, scope, action, resource id, or lifetime. An operation
# binding may also declare `headless_scopes` — a subset of its `invoke_scopes` —
# which is the ONLY authority MintModuleOperationContext grants when the module
# acts with no person present; a binding without it cannot be minted headless.
# One operation binding per module may also declare `source_delegation_scopes`
# — a subset of its `invoke_scopes` — which makes it the binding a person's
# connect of a datasource source delegates to, and is the ONLY authority
# MintSourceOperationContext grants from that delegation.
MODULE_PRINCIPALS=
